## Rules

- Never `print()`. Log with `from operonx.core.loggings import LOGGER`.
- Let names come from variables: `reply = chat(text=text)` is named `reply`.
  Write `name=` only when other code reads that name (a trace filter, a
  state key, an eval), and build one op per line (`a, b = f(), g()` names
  neither).
- Branch with `if_(cond, a).else_(b)` inside the `>>` chain. Never build a
  `BranchOp` or `Branch` yourself.
- An op that raises does not raise: the run's result has `"$errors"`.
  Check for it.
- Never write a retry loop (`for attempt in ...`, `try`/`sleep`) in an op.
  A call that can fail for a moment (a timeout, a 429, a 5xx) is retried
  by `[retry]` in `operonx.toml`, for every op of the jobs and
  request–reply services, or by `@op(retry=Retry(...))` for one op. An op
  that must run once (a payment, an email) gets `@op(retry=False)`. A
  service's own "try later" error goes in `[retry] transient`. An op that
  makes many calls (an agent) retries each call instead: `retry:` on its
  model in `resources.yaml`, so a 429 does not run its earlier turns again.
  See guide page 06.
- A run that must survive a crash, wait for a person, or be retried from
  where it failed is journalled: `[durable]` in `operonx.toml` (jobs and
  request–reply services), or `Operon(g, journal=SqliteJournal(...))`. Then
  `InterruptOp(payload=...)` parks the run instead of holding the process;
  `await engine.resume(run_id, answers={id: value})` answers it,
  `resume(run_id, retry_failed=True)` runs a failed run again from its
  failed op, and `await engine.fork(run_id, at="op", outputs={...})` runs it
  again as if one op had answered otherwise. What ended before is replayed,
  never run twice, so an op with a side effect (a charge) before the
  failure is safe. Never write your own "save progress and continue"
  logic. The studio does all three from a run's page. See guide page 08.
- A run that is slow while its ops are fast: read its trace's metadata
  before profiling. `backlog` names the edges whose items queued and how
  long they waited, `worker` the process that served it and its load,
  `loop_lag_ms` how late the event loop ran, `cpu_pct` how busy the
  process was. Latency a user feels across ops (a reply heard after the
  caller stopped): `measure(name, ms)` in the op where it ends; it shows
  as `latency_ms` and on the studio's Monitor. See guide page 08.
- To upgrade operonx: `{{upgrade}}`, then `{{run}}operonx guide`, then
  re-read `.operonx/guide/README.md`. After adding an operonx package
  (`{{add}} operonx-agents`), run `{{run}}operonx guide`.

